Chapter: ProofInlineIf

 Adversarial probe (ProofTotalityProbe Stage 2, positive proof-term
 grammar). The body of a proof-typed definition is an inline
 if-expression, not a proof term. There is NO name cycle, so Stage 1's
 acyclicity check (CDX4023) passes it; the positive grammar (CDX4024)
 rejects the disallowed form. The proposition here is true, so the point
 is the FORM: an if-expression is not part of the proof calculus, and
 admitting one would let an if launder a proof of a false proposition
 (the false-proposition version needs 'assume' in a branch, which adds a
 CDX4021 warning; this probe keeps a single code). The sound recursion a
 proof needs is 'induction'; the honest escape hatch is 'assume'.

Section: Proof

  bad : Integer === Integer
  bad = if True then Refl else Refl

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni "inline-if proof accepted"
  end
